div><font color="#0f172a">- List the primary neuro-rehab diagnosis first (e.g., stroke, ICH, SCI) including the onset date and surgeries. Explicitly state the patient's current functional deficits.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- Secondary medical problems (e.g., UTI, Spasticity, Sputum, Scalp Wounds, Hyperthyroidism) must be listed as distinct numbered problems (#).&nbsp;</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- For each secondary problem, briefly summarize its current clinical status, treatment day (e.g., Antibiotic Day X), and how it impacts or restricts active rehabilitation.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">4. Plan (P):</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- Group therapies clearly under # Rehabilitation Program (PT/OT/ST) and use concise bullet points.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- Ensure therapy goals and interventions match the functional deficits mentioned in the Objective and Assessment sections.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- Streamline medical and pharmacological plans under concise problem-based headers. Combine sleep medications into a single clear regimen.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">- Keep the language punchy, direct, and actionable for the nursing and therapy staff to respect character constraints.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">Reply with pure text code block only (no markdown formatting, no code blocks).<br></font></div><div><br>Note: use - for styling</div><div></div>